    <slideshow>
        <title>Chapter 1</title>
        <subtitle>MAT 2100: Introduction to Statistics</subtitle>

        <frontmatter>
            <titlepage>
              <event>Grace College</event>
              <date>Last updated <today/></date>
            </titlepage>

        </frontmatter>

        <slide>
          <title>The Jesus Creed</title>                         
          <p>Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God, the Lord is one.</p>
          <p>Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all</p>
          <p>your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength.</p>
          <p>The second is this: Love your neighbor as yourself.</p>
        </slide>

        <section> 
          <title>Section 1.1</title>

          
          <slide>
            <p>
              <term>Statistics</term> is a discipline that guides researchers in 
              collecting, exploring, and drawing conclusions from data.  This course 
              will demonstrate the statistical investigation process for a variety of 
              fields of application.
            </p>
          </slide>
        </section>

        <section> 
          <title>The six steps of a statistical investigation</title>
        
          <slide>
            <p>
              The <term>six steps of a statistical investigation</term> is a framework 
              for learning from data.  The six steps are a guide for collecting and 
              interpreting data in keeping with the scientific method. When research 
              questions are investigated with the six-step method, conclusions are 
              supported by systematic study rather than individual experiences. The six 
              steps are described below.
            </p>
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <p>
              <dl>
                <li>
                  <title>1. Ask a research question.</title>
                  <p>
                    Confirm the question can be addressed by collecting data. Typical 
                    questions involve comparing groups, asking how two characteristics 
                    are related, or surveying people's opinions.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>2. Design a study and collect data.</title>
                  <p>
                    Plan how to select the people or objects to be studied, decide how to 
                    gather the relevant data, and carry out the data collection in a careful, 
                    systematic manner.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>3. Explore the data.</title>
                  <p>
                    Look for patterns that relate to the research question or raise additional 
                    questions.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </slide>

          <slide> 
            <p> 
              <dl> 
                <li>
                  <title>4. Draw inferences beyond the data.</title>
                  <p>
                    Determine whether any findings in the data reflect a genuine tendency and 
                    estimate the strength of that tendency.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>5. Formulate conclusions</title>
                  <p>
                    Consider whether inferences made in step 4 can be generalized to processes 
                    or groups beyond those represented in the study. Identify whether a 
                    cause-and-effect conclusion is warranted.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>6. Look back and ahead.</title>
                  <p>
                    Point out limitations of the study and suggest new studies to build on the findings.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <title> Asking a research question</title>
            <p>State a research question you would be interested in answering.</p>
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <title> What affects the choice to become an organ donor?</title>
            <p>Researchers <url href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1324774">
              Johnson and Goldstein</url> asked 161 volunteer participants to imagine moving to 
              a new state and applying for a driver's license. One part of the application asked 
              whether the participants would agree to be an organ donor. Participants were presented 
              with one of three different forms of the question:
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li> You will not be a donor unless you opt in.</li>
                <li> You will be a donor unless you opt out.</li>
                <li> Choose whether or not to be a donor (neutral).</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </slide>
        </section>

        <section xml:id="sec-Basic-terminology">
          <title>Basic Terminology</title>
          
          <slide>
            <title>Basic Terminology</title>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <term>Data</term> are the values measured or categories recorded on 
                    individual objects of interest.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The <term>observational unit</term> is the individual object on which 
                    data are recorded.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A <term>variable</term> is a characteristic being recorded about the 
                    observational units. 
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <ul>
                      <li>
                        <p>
                          A <term>quantitative variable</term> has numeric values for which 
                          ordinary arithmetic (addition, multiplication) makes sense. 
                        </p>
                      </li>
                      <li>
                        <p>
                          A <term>categorical variable</term> places observational units 
                          into categories.
                        </p>
                      </li>
                    </ul>
                  </p>
                </li>      
                <li>
                  <p>
                    A <term>distribution</term> is the pattern of outcomes of a variable.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>      
            </p>            
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <p>
              The best way to determine if a variable is quantitative or categorical is to ask the 
              question, <q>Can I take the average of this? And would that make sense?</q> For example, 
              I could ask a group of people to tell me what ZIP code they currently live in, and I 
              could technically take the average of those numbers, but it wouldn't make sense to do 
              so. That is because ZIP codes are categorical.
            </p>
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <title>Study design elements</title>
            <p>For the research question you stated at the beginning of the section, identify the 
              observational units and variable(s) you would use,  and describe how you would collect 
              the data. For each variable, indicate whether the variable would be quantitative or 
              categorical.</p>            
          </slide>
        </section>
         
        <section> 
          <title>Section 1.2</title>
          
          <slide>
            <p>In this section, step 3 of the Statistical Investigation Process is further explored. 
              The goal is to describe, measure, and, if possible, explain the patterns of variation 
              (the <em>distribution</em>) in the values of the variable.
            </p>
          </slide>

          
        </section>

        <section xml:id="sec-graphs-quant">
          <title>Graphs for quantitative variables</title>
          
          <slide>
            <slide>
              <p>
                A <term>dotplot</term> is a graph with one dot representing the variable outcome 
                for each observational unit. Dotplots, like the graph of the performance lengths 
                in the animation above, are good for smaller datasets. Another common graph for a 
                quantitative variable is a histogram. A <term>histogram</term> separates the 
                quantitative variable into bins and displays the number of observations in each 
                bin.
              </p>
            </slide>
          </slide>
        </section>

        <section xml:id="sec-describing-distribution">
          <title>Describing the distribution</title>
          
          <slide>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  The <term>shape</term> of a distribution is described by the number of 
                  peaks and whether one side of the distribution tends to stretch out longer 
                  than the other.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The <term>center</term> of a distribution is the middle or typical value.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The <term>variability</term> of a distribution is the spread in the observations.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <term>Unusual observations</term> are observations that do not follow the overall 
                  pattern of the distribution.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </slide>
        </section>
        
        <section> 
          <title>Section 1.3: Preliminary: Exploring random processes</title>
         
         </section>

         <section xml:id="sec-Random-processes">
          <title>Random processes</title>

          <slide>
            <p>  A <term>random process</term> is a repeatable process where the result for an 
              individual attempt is not known in advance but there is a predictable pattern to 
              the results in the long run. Playing <em>Let's Make a Deal</em> is an example of 
              a random process: The result for the next round of the game is uncertain, but if 
              the game is played many, many times, a predictable pattern is expected.  </p>
          </slide>
          
          <slide>
            <p>A <term>simulation</term> is an artificial re-creation of a random process.</p>

          </slide>
         </section>

         <section xml:id="sec-Interpretation-probability">
          <title>Interpretation of probability</title>
          
          <slide>
            <p>
              The <term>probability</term> of an outcome is the long-run proportion of times an 
              outcome from a random process occurs if the process is repeated indefinitely under 
              identical conditions. A probability can be written as a decimal (0.3333), a fraction 
              (1/3), or a percentage (33.33%).
            </p>
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <title>Switch or Stay</title>
            <p>How do you think the probability of winning with the switch strategy will compare to the probability of winning with the stay strategy? Briefly explain your reasoning.</p>
           
          </slide>

          <slide>
            <p>
              The sections in this chapter may have felt unrelated and brief, but they each 
              contributed important components of drawing conclusions from data, including 
              the six-step investigation process, variation in data, and exploring randomness 
              and probability.
            </p>
          </slide>
         </section>


